✅ One-Block Setup for fsie-node1
# Create user if not exists
sudo id fsie-node1 &>/dev/null || sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash fsie-node1

# Ensure correct home ownership
sudo chown -R fsie-node1:fsie-node1 /home/fsie-node1

# Fix home permissions
sudo chmod 700 /home/fsie-node1

# Create .ssh directory safely
sudo mkdir -p /home/fsie-node1/.ssh

# Set correct permissions
sudo chmod 700 /home/fsie-node1/.ssh

# Create authorized_keys file if not exists
sudo touch /home/fsie-node1/.ssh/authorized_keys

# Set correct permissions on key file
sudo chmod 600 /home/fsie-node1/.ssh/authorized_keys

# Final ownership correction
sudo chown -R fsie-node1:fsie-node1 /home/fsie-node1/.ssh

echo "User and SSH setup complete."

🔑 Now Add Public Key

If you want to automatically copy your current user’s public key into fsie-node1:

sudo bash -c "cat ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub >> /home/fsie-node1/.ssh/authorized_keys"


(Only works if you already have a key in ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub.)

🧪 Test Login
ssh fsie-node1@fsie-node1


or from another node:

ssh fsie-node1@<IP-address>

🔒 Why This Works

SSH requires:

Item	Required Permission
/home/user	700
.ssh	700
authorized_keys	600
Ownership	Must belong to the user


